So you know that this is a serious issue.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:32.460
When it comes to what they're made out of, though,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:37.060
all the origins agree that the Transformers are metal and basically mechanical.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.800
So, instead of staying alive with food and water,

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.640
they need the right kind of fuel.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.220
So, thanks a lot, Captain Obvious.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.540
The machine monsters are made out of metal.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:46.020
What about some specifics?

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.100
Well, we actually get the most information about the chemistry of the Transformers,

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.240
at the molecular level, from the movies.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.000
Specifically, "Age of Extinction".

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.440
They even take the time to name the Transformers' material

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.200
"TRANSFORMIUM"

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.260
Yeah, SUPER creative job there, everybody.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:22.360
Now, if only you'd spend as much time building your fictional world as you did researching the laws around

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.120
relationships with minors.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.200
This is illegal,

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:25.700
she's a minor!

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.600
We're protected by the Romeo and Juliet laws.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.460
We've got a preexisting juvenile foundation

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:33.880
statute two seven zero

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:36.240
five dash three

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:37.420
Romeo and Juliet, huh?

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.000
And on that note, yeah, yeah, I know.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.180
It pains me to go the Michael Bay movies.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.940
Michael Bay's Transformers are practically unwatchable and offensive,

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.820
taking a lot of liberties with the Transformers lore.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.460
His vocal processors were damaged in battle.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:58.940
But, the fact of the matter is that the movies give us the most concrete, scientific clues about what these guys are actually made out of.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:02.140
So if we're going to take a look at the real-world technology behind these things,

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:04.140
well, this is gonna be the best route to take.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.200
So this theory is gonna focus on transformium,

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:10.140
and, from there, we're going to see how close we are to making our own Optimus Prime.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:13.120
And the fact of the matter is the technology is really cool,

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.160
so let's all agree to get along here in the name of science.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:16.880
Okay?

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:17.460
Okay.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:22.220
So, in Age of Extinction, humans have gotten pretty sick of alien robots destroying their cities when they fight each other.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:25.420
So the U.S. government starts trying to make their own Transformers.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.240
The scientists crack the code of how to work with transformium

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:28.740
and BOOM!

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:29.520
Man-made Transformer!

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.820
So how far am I from being able to make my own bodyguard slash camper van?

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:36.160
Well, let's take a look at what we know about Transformium.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.240
ROLL OUT!

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:37.740
the tape.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:39.540
A rare earth metal,

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.220
molecularly unstable,

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.740
industrial uses--none.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.120
So according to Tobias Fünke's evil twin

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:50.460
we're looking at a metal that's unstable, rare, and isn't for industrial purposes.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.040
We also know a few things from the Transformers themselves.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.020
Transformium oughtta be pretty light-weight,

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:56.300
since some of the Transformers can fly,

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.140
which wouldn't happen if they were made out of, say, lead.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:01.120
They also enter and exit Earth's atmosphere,

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.740
so this metal needs to have a high melting point.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:11.660
Specifically, entering the atmosphere causes objects to reach 1,477 degrees Celsius, or 2691 degrees Fahrenheit

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:12.900
for all of us in the good 'ol

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.220
U.S. of A,

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:17.660
where our independence is dictated by how difficult our measurement  system is.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:21.440
So, the melting point of whatever we're using would have to be above that.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:24.120
Is there a substance on Earth that can check off all these boxes?

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:28.860
Well, a quick perusal of all the usual suspects on the periodic table tells us no.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.180
But then, what's this?

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:33.520
A group of elements called rare earth metals, since they are

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:34.580
--big shocker--

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:35.580
RARE.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:40.600
Man, it looks like chemists are just as bad at naming things as the writers for Age of Extinction.

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:42.640
Maybe I should stop giving them such a hard time.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:43.580
Search the property!

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:45.500
What do you mean, "search the property"?
You don't have a warrant.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:48.660
Nope. Totally justified.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.800
Now, the best analog for transformium would actually be lightest of the bunch--

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:53.720
scandium.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.020
Atomic number twenty one.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.880
It's lightweight, with a density similar to aluminum,

00:05:57.880 --> 00:05:59.960
so flying around like Starscream wouldn't be an issue.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:04.020
And it has a melting point of 1,540 degrees Celsius,

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.720
well above our limit for objects entering the atmosphere.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:13.780
This ensures that Optimus and the gang don't pull an Alex Mack and turn into a puddle of metallic goo each time they enter the planet's atmosphere.

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:17.060
We don't use scandium for industrial use because it's so rare,

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:18.880
just like we hear about in the movie,

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:23.580
and scandium even looks like the sample of transformium that we see in Age of Extinction.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:27.280
On a chemical level, if we were to start making our own Transformers,

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:32.060
scandium would actually be a really good, really accurate candidate.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:33.360
And, while that's all well and good,

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:34.880
it's far from the whole equation,

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.540
because the big challenge of transformium isn't the chemistry;

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:39.140
it's the physics.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:43.640
The problem with creating Optimus Prime isn't creating something that looks like his truck form,

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:44.940
that's just a truck.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:48.900
We want a substance that can mutate itself into practically anything,

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:49.840
and, as it turns out,

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:55.600
advances in robotics are making transformium look less like a pipe dream and more like an inevitability.

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:57.840
In fact, the building blocks are already here.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.300
For example, we already have machines that can react to their environments as necessary.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:05.400
These are called self configuring modular robots,

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:09.120
and they can change shapes, walk, and, in some cases, even fly.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.120
Just take a look at the M-TRAN3:

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:13.580
an example of an self configuring modular robot

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:17.740
made of interlocking parts that can change its configuration and movement at will.

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:22.320
As you can see here, it's arranged itself into a long chain that moves by undulating like a caterpillar.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:23.880
But, it can also form a lattice,

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:25.020
grow legs,

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:26.160
even walk away!

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:27.400
Sure, it's rudimentary,

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:32.720
but let's face it, it already looks a lot better than those walking domino blocks that we saw in Interstellar.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:35.540
In both the real world and the Transformers universe,

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:39.920
the robot is changing its appearance to best suit the purpose it needs to fulfill.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:42.500
Now, you might think this thing is slow, unimpressive,

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:47.120
and looks like a miniature plastic AT-AT from Empire Strikes Back than a Transformer,

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:52.360
but keep in mind that the M-TRAN3 debuted at a robotics expo in 2005.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:57.260
In 2005, the Nintendo Wii, and the iPhone one were things of the future.

00:07:57.260 --> 00:07:59.380
More recently, we've had M-BLOCKS,

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:03.600
small cubes that self-assemble without legs, wheels, or any external parts,

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.480
just cubes that can hop around and assemble on their own with nothing more than magnets.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:10.500
Still, if we're looking for something like transformium,

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:15.940
we would need robots that are smaller, faster, and more versatile than these self configuring modular robots.

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:19.160
Transformium, as we see in the movie, is programmable;

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:20.680
it has a memory.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:24.060
Programmable matter might seem way out of our collective human league,

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:30.560
but scientists are actually developing exactly that in a branch of nanotechnology known as claytronics.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:33.980
Think of claytronics as a mix of robotics and computer science.

00:08:33.980 --> 00:08:40.180
Claytronics uses a system of very small programmable computers known as claytronic atoms, or catoms,

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:45.760
working in conjunction with each other to create three-dimensional objects that the user can interact with.

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:49.060
So imagine you're Skyping or FaceTiming with someone a few years in the future.

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:52.340
Right now, all you're gonna get is a two-dimensional feed of the person you're talking to.

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:57.700
But claytronics would make it possible for your device to do a full body scan of the person you're talking to,

00:08:57.700 --> 00:08:59.660
recreate them out of catoms,

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:04.660
and then put a three-dimensional version of the person right next to you in your very room.

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:06.800
And then, when the catoms are done being your friend,

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:10.600
they could potentially transform back into a couch or a table or television.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:13.200
The possibilities truly are endless here

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.260
Y'know those cool nanobots from Big Hero 6?

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:15.760
Yeah.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:16.320
It's kinda like that.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:17.320
And here's the kicker--

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:20.140
even though it seems like this kind of technology should be decades,

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:21.860
if not centuries, away,

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:24.400
experts think we're pretty close right now.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:29.740
An article from the Institute of Ethics &amp; Emerging Technologies in 2014 cites that researchers

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:35.460
at Intel Labs are projecting basic claytronic products for commercial use by the year 2020.

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:39.060
And researchers at Carnegie Mellon University are making noteworthy progress.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:44.800
They've produced electromagnetic catoms which are able to disassemble and reconfigure themselves in a span of

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:46.600
one tenth of a second.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:52.700
So just give it a few more years and shape-shifting programmable matter just like transformium could be

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:54.800
in your very living room!

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:56.060
Or I guess, in this case,

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:57.320
in your garage.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:09:59.740
But then this all begs one final question.

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:01.220
If we can make transformium,

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:03.820
then wouldn't actual Transformers be right behind?

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:07.880
In fact, all you loyal fans of Film Theory will remember our episode about Terminator's Skynet,

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:13.060
where we learn that artificial intelligence is projected to advance to the point that computers become self-aware

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:15.680
maybe as soon as 2045.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:19.400
So if we create shape-shifting robot computers through claytronics by 2020,

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:21.840
and true A.I. by 2045,

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:24.840
it would only be a matter of time before they become our robotic overlords, right?

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:30.980
Well, there's still one fatal flaw in building robots that can transform and combine to become more than meets the eye

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:34.460
and it's not in the robots themselves or their A.I.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:38.300
In fact, the biggest problem is also the most stupidest.

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:41.080
And yes, I know I did that grammatically incorrect for effect.

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:42.460
I know it's more stupider.

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:45.080
The issue, believe it or not, is this:

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:47.600
That's right, friends.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:53.540
There's one big issue holding back the advent of Transformers in the real world and it's a problem that we've faced all our lives:

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:55.540
Batteries aren't included.

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:59.480
Yes, from the days of your battery-draining Super Scope Six

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:02.820
to your Transformers Megatron walkie talkie radio

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:06.240
to the line you say yourself every single day,

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:07.840
"I need to go charge my phone",

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:10.440
batteries are what are holding us back the most.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:15.640
Even though our circuits are going microscale and our computers are getting more and more powerful,

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:17.740
we can't say the same about batteries.

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:22.680
In the past 60 years, we've seen a trillionfold increase in computing power--

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:23.640
that's no exaggeration,

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:27.040
a literal trillionfold increase in computing power--

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:32.580
but since 2007, phone battery life has only improved by 22%.

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:36.500
And the size of the thing is still relatively huge compared to the size of the phone.

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:38.740
The problem isn't creating Transformers,

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:40.220
it's powering them.

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:46.080
Entirely new power source systems just don't exist for these sorts of super-advanced robot-A.I. hybrids,

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:49.760
and it's standing between us and the Transformer revolution, my friend.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:55.060
Well, at least until they become our overlords and start harvesting human electrical potential to fuel them like the Matrix.

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:55.920
I mean, what?

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:57.020
But hey,

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:59.000
that's just a theory.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.120
A FILM THEORY!

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:02.960
And...cut!

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:06.580
Seriously though, we all know that the Matrix human battery thing is complete fiction,

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:08.780
so, no need to leave the note in the comments.

